// The multi-argument shape of the class/function name collision.
//
// This one did not even parse. A statement-position `widget(w, 9)` was routed to
// parseVarDecl because `widget` names a class (Rea identifiers are
// case-insensitive); parseVarDecl consumed the name as a type and stopped at
// '(', so the leftover `(w, 9)` was re-parsed as a parenthesized expression and
// died on the comma with "Unexpected token COMMA ',' at line N" -- a parse error
// naming a construct the program never wrote.
//
// Both arguments are printed so this fixture fails if the call is dispatched
// with the wrong arity or the wrong values (the collision previously made the
// bare name mean the constructor, which takes an implicit receiver first).
class Widget { int count; Widget(int v) { my.count = v; } }

void widget(Widget t, int extra) { writeln("free fn sees ", t.count, " ", extra); }

Widget w = new Widget(5);
widget(w, 9);
writeln(w.count);
